<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Aisin Gioro</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aisin Gioro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Aisin Gioro is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> clan that ruled the Later Jin dynasty from 1616, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> from 1636 to 1912 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a> from 1932 to 1945. Under the Ming its members were chiefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jianzhou-jurchens">Jianzhou Jurchens</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> created the clan as part of his reorganisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> society in 1601, naming the descendants of his father Taksi Aisin, 'gold'. It had no fixed rule of succession: an emperor named his heir in a secret edict. Princely titles fell by one rank with each generation, except for twelve 'iron-cap' peerages that passed on undiminished. The clan had 29,000 members when the Qing fell in 1912, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> kept his title in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a> until 1924.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Aisin Gioro traced their ancestry to Bukūri Yongšon, a warrior of the thirteenth century. Emperor Hongtaiji claimed that he was conceived by a virgin birth: a magpie dropped a red fruit near the heavenly maiden Fekulen, who ate it and became pregnant. The same story belongs to another <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> clan, the Hurha, and the real ancestor of the Aisin Gioro was Möngke Temür, a chieftain of the Odoli tribe under the Mongol Yuan Empire.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> reorganised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> society in 1601, the Gioro clan was partitioned. Descendants of his father Taksi became Aisin, while other Gioros took names such as Irgen Gioro or Hūlun Gioro. The people the clan ruled were ethnically mixed, and in 1635 all of them were designated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> replaced small hunting groups with banners of some 7,500 warriors and their households, identified by coloured flags. In time there were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a>, Mongol and Chinese banners, eight of each, and by 1648 fewer than one sixth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">bannermen</a> were of Manchu ancestry. The conquest of the Ming was thus won by a multiethnic army led by Manchu nobles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> generals.</p><p>Marriage served as policy. Aisin Gioro princesses were given to Mongol princes and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> officers, including sons of the generals Sun Sike, Geng Jimao, Shang Kexi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a>, to bring them into alliance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorgon">Dorgon</a> and Jirgalang acted as regents early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunzhi-emperor">Shunzhi Emperor</a>'s reign, and in 1861 the regents Duanhua, Sushun and Zaiyuan were ousted in the Xinyou Coup.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The core branch numbered 378 people in 1660 and 29,292 in 1915, a smaller increase than the Ming house of Zhu, which had 200,000 members by its fall. After the Qing fell most members took Chinese surnames such as Jin, Zhao, Ai or Luo. Later heads of the house were named under a succession law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> approved in 1937: Pujie held the position from 1967 to 1994, followed by Jin Youzhi until 2015 and then Jin Yuzhang. Some 300,000 to 400,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> bear the surname Aisin Gioro.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Aisin Gioro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
